    "math_academy_id": "q-81064",
    "knowledge_point_id": "fce4cd5a-537a-5eb4-a610-59d2bad95a89",
    "knowledge_point": "Deducing the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial Given the Graph of a Function",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/assets/185b5924c542bb9f396fe826aac141256a13b967c45c47afc87ca07445df062f.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=-1?$",
    "worked_solution": "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(-1)+{f}^{′}(-1)(x+1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-1)}{2}(x+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-1)>0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(-1)>0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)<0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=6+4(x+1)-5(x+1)^{2}.\n$$",
